WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:02.835
In this lesson,

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:06.956
you will learn the most natural way to use
Zonal: Snapping with the mouse.

00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:08.770
You drag windows anyway.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:12.246
Holding one key turns that drag into a
precise placement,

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:14.746
and you see every Zone before you choose.

00:00:16.121 --> 00:00:19.791
This Layout has three Zones. Let's put
this window into one of them.

00:00:20.352 --> 00:00:25.164
Start dragging the window by its title
bar, the top edge, like you always do.

00:00:25.164 --> 00:00:26.913
Nothing special happens yet.

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:29.467
Now hold Shift, and keep holding it.

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:33.848
Watch the screen: the overlay appears,
with the Zones and their numbers.

00:00:34.209 --> 00:00:36.923
The overlay only shows while you hold
Shift,

00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:39.576
so an ordinary drag stays an ordinary
drag.

00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:44.319
Move the pointer over the Zone you want.
The Zone under the pointer lights up.

00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:46.176
That is where the window will go.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.506
You do not need to aim precisely.

00:00:48.506 --> 00:00:53.032
If the pointer is in the gap between two
Zones, Zonal picks the nearest one.

00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.315
Let go of the mouse button.

00:00:55.315 --> 00:00:59.066
The window Snaps: it takes the exact size
and place of the Zone.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.958
Let's do it once more with the other
window.

00:01:01.958 --> 00:01:04.356
Drag, hold Shift, choose a Zone, and let
go.

00:01:04.836 --> 00:01:07.793
Sometimes one window needs more room for a
while,

00:01:07.793 --> 00:01:10.388
like a wide spreadsheet or a long
timeline.

00:01:10.388 --> 00:01:12.681
You do not need a new Layout for that.

00:01:13.578 --> 00:01:18.439
Hold Shift, then add Control. Now every
Zone you cross joins the selection.

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:20.124
Watch both Zones light up.

00:01:20.491 --> 00:01:23.676
Let go, and the window covers all the
Zones you selected,

00:01:23.676 --> 00:01:25.409
including the gap between them.

00:01:26.046 --> 00:01:29.952
Changed your mind halfway? Release Shift
before you release the mouse button.

00:01:30.307 --> 00:01:34.778
The overlay disappears, and the window
stays exactly where you drop it,

00:01:34.778 --> 00:01:35.974
like a normal drag.

00:01:35.974 --> 00:01:37.863
Pressing Escape does the same.

00:01:39.502 --> 00:01:42.383
Some examples of when the drag is the
right gesture.

00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:47.803
Your morning setup: open mail, calendar
and chat, and Snap each into its Zone.

00:01:47.803 --> 00:01:49.168
It takes ten seconds.

00:01:49.518 --> 00:01:53.944
While coding, drag the documentation next
to your editor, never on top of it.

00:01:54.294 --> 00:01:56.024
A spreadsheet that is too wide?

00:01:56.024 --> 00:02:00.040
Span it over two Zones while you work on
it, then Snap it back into one.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.585
To recap. Hold Shift while you drag, and
let go over a Zone.

00:02:05.585 --> 00:02:07.916
Add Control to cover several Zones.

00:02:07.916 --> 00:02:11.378
And release Shift first, or press Escape,
to cancel.

00:02:11.728 --> 00:02:14.610
Try it now with two or three of your own
windows.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.904
After a few Snaps, it becomes a reflex.

00:02:18.454 --> 00:02:22.500
In the next lesson, you will learn to Snap
a window without dragging at all,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.671
from its green button.
